// BranchCleanup — the Target-tier CFG cleanup (EC11), on FULLY REGISTER-ALLOCATED PHYSICAL OPS.
// Shared by every backend that allocates registers (x64 and arm64); wasm consumes Std directly and
// never reaches this tier.
//
// Four transforms, all of them local to one function's block layout:
//
//   1. JUMP ELISION       — a block whose terminator is the ISA's unconditional branch to the block
//                           laid down immediately after it loses that branch. Control reaches the
//                           target by falling off the end.
//   2. CONDITIONAL INVERSION — a conditional branch to the NEXT block followed by an unconditional
//                           branch to the else target becomes the COMPLEMENTED condition to the else
//                           target plus a fall-through. Two instructions become one.
//   3. JUMP THREADING     — a block whose ONLY op is an unconditional branch is bypassed: every
//                           branch that named it is re-pointed at its target.
//   4. UNREACHABLE-BLOCK ELIMINATION — the blocks the threading (or the parser's `dead`
//                           continuations) left with no path from the entry are dropped.
//
// ⭐⭐ WHY IT RUNS WHERE IT DOES — AFTER `applyAllocation`, AND NOT ONE PASS EARLIER. THIS IS THE
// WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT FOR TRANSFORM 3.
//
// SSA destruction places a phi edge's parallel copies ON THE EDGE, which for a critical edge means
// inside the `critsplit` block that was spliced onto it. BEFORE that runs, a block whose only op is
// a branch is not empty — it is an edge that is ABOUT TO RECEIVE COPIES, and threading it away would
// delete moves that had not been written yet. After `applyAllocation`, "the block's only op is a
// branch" is literally true and permanent: a `critsplit` that received edge copies holds `mov`s and
// is correctly left alone, and one that received none is genuinely empty and is correctly threaded
// away. That asymmetry is the reason for the placement, and it is not visible from the pass itself.
//
// The same placement answers the phi model: `applyAllocation` ends by calling `clearPhiModel`, so
// every block's `blockArgs` and `branchEdges` are already EMPTY here. There is no phi metadata for
// this pass to keep consistent — the ops are the only remaining statement of the CFG.
//
// ⛔ AND IT DOES NOT REORDER BLOCKS. It only DELETES — a branch, or a block nothing can reach — so
// every surviving block keeps its position relative to every other. That matters because a block
// with no terminator op (a parser `dead` continuation) reaches the backend relying on physically
// falling into its neighbour, and the ops-derived CFG cannot see that edge. See
// `dropUnreachableBlocks`, which is the one place that hole is closed.
//
// ISA-neutrality is not an accident of the transforms: every question this pass asks is answered by
// a shared roster. `unconditionalBranchTargetOf` classifies both ISAs' terminators, `retargetControlOp`
// covers both ISAs' conditional branches and both jump tables, `Terminator.fallthrough` is a layout
// fact rather than an encoding, and the single per-ISA fact — a condition code's complement — is
// `invertX64Cond` / `invertArm64Cond` beside the enums they read (see `invertedCondBranchOnto`).

// The pass, over every function in the module. Called from `buildX64Backend` and `buildArm64Backend`
// between `allocateRegisters` and the frame pass: the frame pass then sees the SURVIVING blocks, so
// a callee-saved register or a `call` that only unreachable code used stops being preserved.
export function cleanUpBranches(module TargetModule, project Project)
	let probe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.branchCleanup)
	logDebug(LogCategory.codegen, message: function() gives "Branch cleanup: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage — the discipline `insertPrologueEpilogue` states.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		cleanUpFunctionBranches(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("cleanUpBranches: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'

	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.branchCleanup, probe: probe)
end 'cleanUpBranches'

// ⭐ THE ORDER OF THE FOUR IS LOAD-BEARING, and it splits at one line.
//
// Threading and reachability both need a CFG, and the CFG is derived FROM THE OPS — a block's second
// successor is its conditional branch's target and its first is its terminator's. Transforms 1 and 2
// DELETE that terminator, which makes the else-edge unreadable from the ops (it becomes a layout fact
// instead). So every question that needs the CFG is asked first, and the elision is last — after
// which nothing in the compiler builds a CFG for this function again.
function cleanUpFunctionBranches(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	// The skeleton read off the ops, exactly as the allocator builds it — this pass derives no CFG of
	// its own. Locals are LAYOUT positions (`buildFuncTopology` numbers them in `func.blockRefs`
	// order), which is what makes "the physically next block" answerable as `local + 1`.
	let topo = buildFuncTopology(module, func: func)
	if topo.numBlocks == 0 'noBlocks'
		return
	end 'noBlocks'

	let resolved = resolveJumpChains(module, topo: topo)
	threadResolvedEdges(module, topo: topo, resolved: resolved)
	dropUnreachableBlocks(module, func: func, topo: topo, resolved: resolved)
	elideFallthroughBranches(module, func: func)
end 'cleanUpFunctionBranches'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 3 — jump threading
// ==========================================================================

// `resolved[L]` — the block a branch naming local `L` must be re-pointed at. `L` itself for every
// block that stays; the far end of a chain of empty branch-only blocks otherwise.
//
// Resolved with PATH COMPRESSION over an explicit path stack rather than a repeat-until-stable
// sweep: a chain of K forwarding blocks converges in K rounds of the naive form, which is quadratic
// in exactly the shape this pass exists to clean up (the inliners emit runs of them).
function resolveJumpChains(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology) returns CfgIndexArray
	let blockCount = topo.numBlocks

	// Classified in ONE pass over the blocks, so the chain walk below dereferences no block at all.
	var forwards = BoolArray.create()
	forwards.resize(blockCount)
	var forwardTargetOf = zeroColumn(blockCount)

	// ⭐ LOCAL 0 — THE ENTRY — IS NEVER FORWARDED THROUGH, whatever its ops. It is the block the frame
	// pass puts the prologue in, so a predecessor re-pointed PAST it would enter the function without a
	// frame. (The parser gives a loop its own header rather than branching back to the entry, so this
	// costs nothing today; it is the fact that must not have to be re-derived if that ever changes.)
	for local in 1 upto blockCount 'eachBlock'
		let target = emptyJumpTargetOf(module, topo: topo, local: local)
		if target == local 'staysPut'
			continue
		end 'staysPut'

		try forwards.set(local, value: true) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwards.set OOB at {local}")
		try forwardTargetOf.set(local, value: target) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwardTargetOf.set OOB at {local}")
	end 'eachBlock'

	// `blockCount` is one past every valid local, so it can never collide with a real answer.
	let unresolved = blockCount
	var resolved = filledColumn(blockCount, value: unresolved)
	var onPath = BoolArray.create()
	onPath.resize(blockCount)
	var path = CfgIndexArray.create()

	for start in 0 upto blockCount 'eachStart'
		if (try resolved.get(start) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {start}")) != unresolved 'alreadyAnswered'
			continue
		end 'alreadyAnswered'

		path.clear()
		var cur = start
		var answer = unresolved
		while answer == unresolved 'followChain'
			// The three facts about `cur`, read up front: a `try` may not sit bare in an `if` condition
			// (E3124), and reading them together keeps the chain below a plain three-way test.
			let forwardsHere = try forwards.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwards.get OOB at {cur}")
			let answerHere = try resolved.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {cur}")
			let onPathHere = try onPath.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.get OOB at {cur}")

			if not forwardsHere 'chainEnds'
				answer = cur
			end 'chainEnds' else if answerHere != unresolved 'answerKnown'
				answer = answerHere
			end 'answerKnown' else if onPathHere 'cycle'
				// A CYCLE of empty branch blocks is an infinite loop with no exit, so there is nothing to
				// forward it to. It resolves to the block the walk RE-ENTERED, which therefore stays —
				// threading the whole cycle away would delete the loop.
				answer = cur
			end 'cycle' else 'follow'
				try onPath.set(cur, value: true) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.set OOB at {cur}")
				path.push(cur)
				cur = try forwardTargetOf.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwardTargetOf.get OOB at {cur}")
			end 'follow'
		end 'followChain'

		for local in path 'compress'
			try resolved.set(local, value: answer) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.set OOB at {local}")
			try onPath.set(local, value: false) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.set OOB at {local}")
		end 'compress'

		// `start` is on the path only when it forwards; a block that ends the chain was never pushed.
		if (try resolved.get(start) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {start}")) == unresolved 'startEndsTheChain'
			try resolved.set(start, value: answer) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.set OOB at {start}")
		end 'startEndsTheChain'
	end 'eachStart'

	return resolved
end 'resolveJumpChains'

// The local a block forwards control to when it is an EMPTY unconditional branch — no body ops at
// all, no conditional branch, just the terminator. Answers `local` itself for every other block, and
// for a branch to ITSELF (an empty infinite loop, which has nothing to forward to).
//
// "No body ops" is the whole test, and after `applyAllocation` it means what it says: a `critsplit`
// block that received this edge's parallel copies holds them as body ops and is refused here, while
// one that received none is genuinely empty. See this file's header.
function emptyJumpTargetOf(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, local CfgIndex) returns CfgIndex
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: local)
	if block.opRefs.count() > 0 or block.hasCondBranchOp() 'carriesWork'
		return local
	end 'carriesWork'

	return match unconditionalBranchTargetOf(module, block: block) 'terminator'
		none gives local
		blockId(target) gives localOfBlockId(topo, blockId: target)
	end 'terminator'
end 'emptyJumpTargetOf'

// Re-point every edge whose target resolved elsewhere, through the one edge-retargeting
// implementation in the compiler (`retargetControlOp`, SsaDestruction) — which covers the `jcc`, the
// `jmp`, the arm64 branches AND both multiway jump tables, so no edge kind can be silently missed.
//
// COLLECT FIRST, THEN WRITE: `retargetControlOp` writes `module.ops`, which no live borrow of a
// block out of `module.blocks` may overlap (E3070). It is also `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc`'s
// discipline for the second reason — the reads classify against ONE consistent CFG, and each
// redirect is independent of the others.
function threadResolvedEdges(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, resolved CfgIndexArray)
	var predRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
	var oldTargets = BlockIdArray.create()
	var newTargets = BlockIdArray.create()

	// ⚠ A SUCCESSOR MAY APPEAR TWICE IN ONE BLOCK'S LIST. `collectBlockSuccessorIds` pushes the
	// conditional branch's then-target and the terminator's else-target without deduping, so a two-way
	// branch whose two arms name one block lists it twice — and the SECOND redirect would find no edge
	// left to move and hit `retargetControlOp`'s no-match panic. Marked and cleared per block rather
	// than scanned: a dense jump table's successor list is long, and a membership scan over it would
	// make this quadratic in the arm count.
	var seen = BoolArray.create()
	seen.resize(topo.numBlocks)

	for local in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachPred'
		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(local) 'eachSucc'
			let succ = topo.succ.neighbour(local, k: k)
			let alreadyListed = try seen.get(succ) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.get OOB at {succ}")
			if alreadyListed 'duplicateEdge'
				continue
			end 'duplicateEdge'
			try seen.set(succ, value: true) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.set OOB at {succ}")

			let target = try resolved.get(succ) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: resolved.get OOB at {succ}")
			if target == succ 'edgeUnchanged'
				continue
			end 'edgeUnchanged'

			predRefs.push(try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(local) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {local}"))
			oldTargets.push(blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: succ).id)
			newTargets.push(blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: target).id)
		end 'eachSucc'

		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(local) 'clearSeen'
			try seen.set(topo.succ.neighbour(local, k: k), value: false) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.set OOB while clearing")
		end 'clearSeen'
	end 'eachPred'

	for k in 0 upto predRefs.count() 'eachRedirect'
		retargetControlOp(module, predRef: try predRefs.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: predRefs.get OOB at {k}"), oldTarget: try oldTargets.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: oldTargets.get OOB at {k}"), newTarget: try newTargets.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: newTargets.get OOB at {k}"))
	end 'eachRedirect'
end 'threadResolvedEdges'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 4 — unreachable-block elimination
// ==========================================================================

// Drop every block with no path from the entry — the empty branch blocks the threading above just
// orphaned, plus whatever the parser's `dead` continuations already were. Survivors keep their
// relative order, so nothing that depends on layout moves.
//
// ⚠⚠ A BLOCK WITH NO TERMINATOR OP FALLS THROUGH PHYSICALLY, AND THE OPS-DERIVED CFG CANNOT SEE
// THAT EDGE. `Terminator.dead` / `Terminator.unset` name no op, so `collectBlockSuccessorIds` gives
// such a block NO successors — its real successor is whatever is laid down next. That hole never
// mattered while nothing removed blocks; it matters now, because dropping the block a live one falls
// into would silently re-point it at whatever survived further down. So the walk adds the physical
// edge, and it is added WITHOUT going through `resolved`: a fall-through is not a branch, threading
// never moved it, and the block it names must survive verbatim.
function dropUnreachableBlocks(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, topo FuncTopology, resolved CfgIndexArray)
	var reachable = BoolArray.create()
	reachable.resize(topo.numBlocks)
	var stack = CfgIndexArray.create()

	// The entry is local 0 and is reachable by definition — it is also the block that must stay first
	// (the prologue goes in it), which the ascending rebuild below preserves.
	try reachable.set(0, value: true) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.set OOB at the entry block")
	stack.push(0)

	while stack.count() > 0 'walk'
		let u = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: stack.pop on non-empty failed")

		// The edges as they stand AFTER the threading above: every branch target goes through `resolved`.
		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(u) 'eachSucc'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, local: try resolved.get(topo.succ.neighbour(u, k: k)) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: resolved.get OOB"))
		end 'eachSucc'

		if (not blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: u).hasTerminatorOp()) and u + 1 < topo.numBlocks 'fallsThroughPhysically'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, local: u + 1)
		end 'fallsThroughPhysically'
	end 'walk'

	var kept = BlockRefArray.create()
	for local in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let survives = try reachable.get(local) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.get OOB at {local}")
		if survives 'keep'
			kept.push(try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(local) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {local}"))
		end 'keep'
	end 'eachBlock'
	func.blockRefs = kept
end 'dropUnreachableBlocks'

// Mark a block reachable and queue it, once. Both columns are the caller's.
function markReachable(reachable BoolArray, stack CfgIndexArray, local CfgIndex)
	let seenBefore = try reachable.get(local) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.get OOB at {local}")
	if seenBefore 'alreadySeen'
		return
	end 'alreadySeen'

	try reachable.set(local, value: true) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.set OOB at {local}")
	stack.push(local)
end 'markReachable'

// ==========================================================================
// Transforms 1 and 2 — jump elision and conditional inversion
// ==========================================================================

// The layout walk, on the FINAL block list. Runs last, and nothing derives a CFG for this function
// afterwards — see `cleanUpFunctionBranches` for why that ordering is not optional.
function elideFallthroughBranches(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	// The LAST block has no physical successor, so it has nothing to fall through into. The count is
	// at least 1 here — `cleanUpFunctionBranches` returns for a function with no blocks, and the drop
	// above always keeps the entry — so the subtraction cannot leave the index type's range.
	for i in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() - 1 'eachBlock'
		let nextRef = try func.blockRefs.get(i + 1) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRefs.get OOB at {i + 1}")
		let nextId = (try module.blocks.get(nextRef) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRef {nextRef} not in module.blocks")).id
		elideBranchInto(module, blockRef: try func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}"), nextId: nextId)
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'elideFallthroughBranches'

// One block's elision, against the id of the block that will be laid down immediately after it.
// A terminator that is not the ISA's unconditional branch — a `ret`, a multiway jump table, an
// op-less `dead` slot — carries nothing to elide.
function elideBranchInto(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, nextId BlockId)
	let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("elideBranchInto: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
	match unconditionalBranchTargetOf(module, block: block) 'terminator'
		none then return
		blockId(elseTarget) then elideAgainstNext(module, blockRef: blockRef, elseTarget: elseTarget, nextId: nextId)
	end 'terminator'
end 'elideBranchInto'

// The two transforms, with the terminator's target in hand.
function elideAgainstNext(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, elseTarget BlockId, nextId BlockId)
	if elseTarget == nextId 'fallsIntoElse'
		// TRANSFORM 1. Whether or not the block also has a conditional branch: that branch names the
		// then-edge and is untouched, and the else-edge is now the fall-through it always was in the
		// emitted layout.
		elideTerminatorIn(module, blockRef: blockRef)
		return
	end 'fallsIntoElse'

	// TRANSFORM 2.
	match invertibleCondBranchIn(module, blockRef: blockRef) 'condSlot'
		none then return
		opIndex(condOpIndex) then invertBranchAndElide(module, blockRef: blockRef, condOpIndex: condOpIndex, nextId: nextId, elseTarget: elseTarget)
	end 'condSlot'
end 'elideAgainstNext'

// The op index of a block whose conditional half is a candidate for inversion, or `none`. Two
// STRUCTURAL things must hold, and the second is the one that is not obvious:
//
//   • the block has a conditional branch at all;
//   • ⚠ that branch is the block's LAST BODY OP. An op sitting BETWEEN it and the terminator runs
//     only when the conditional branch is NOT taken, so inverting the pair would move it onto the
//     other path — a silent wrong answer. `IrModule.appendOp` refuses to append past a recorded
//     conditional branch, but the live-range splitter mints its ops through its own append and
//     bypasses that guard (see `assertCondBranchIndicesComplete`), so this is CHECKED, never assumed.
//
// Whether it TARGETS the next block is decided where the op is read, so the branch is dereferenced
// exactly once — see `invertCondBranchOntoElse`.
function invertibleCondBranchIn(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef) returns CondBranch
	let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invertibleCondBranchIn: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
	let bodyCount = block.opRefs.count()
	if bodyCount == 0 or not block.hasCondBranchOp() 'noConditionalHalf'
		return CondBranch.none
	end 'noConditionalHalf'

	let condOpIndex = block.condBranchIndex()
	let lastBodyOp = try block.opRefs.get(bodyCount - 1) otherwise panic("invertibleCondBranchIn: opRefs.get OOB at {bodyCount - 1}")
	return CondBranch.opIndex(condOpIndex) if condOpIndex == lastBodyOp else CondBranch.none
end 'invertibleCondBranchIn'

// Invert the block's conditional branch onto the else target, and delete the terminator if it did.
function invertBranchAndElide(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, condOpIndex OpIndex, nextId BlockId, elseTarget BlockId)
	if invertCondBranchOntoElse(module, blockRef: blockRef, condOpIndex: condOpIndex, nextId: nextId, elseTarget: elseTarget) 'inverted'
		elideTerminatorIn(module, blockRef: blockRef)
	end 'inverted'
end 'invertBranchAndElide'

// THE ONE PLACE THE CONDITIONAL BRANCH IS REWRITTEN. False when it names anything but the physically
// next block, which is the case where there is no fall-through to trade the terminator for.
//
// The block is read for the target and let go before the write: `module.blocks.get` hands back a
// borrow of `module`, and `module.ops.set` writes that same storage (E3070).
function invertCondBranchOntoElse(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, condOpIndex OpIndex, nextId BlockId, elseTarget BlockId) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(condOpIndex) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: conditional branch op {condOpIndex} not in module.ops")
	let thenTarget = condBranchTargetOf(op, block: try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks"))
	if thenTarget != nextId 'thenIsNotNext'
		return false
	end 'thenIsNotNext'

	try module.ops.set(condOpIndex, value: invertedCondBranchOnto(op, targetBlockId: elseTarget, condOpIndex: condOpIndex)) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: ops.set OOB at {condOpIndex}")
	return true
end 'invertCondBranchOntoElse'

// The conditional branch that is TAKEN exactly when `op` is NOT, aimed at `targetBlockId`.
//
// ⭐ EVERY PAIR HERE IS AN EXACT FLAG-LEVEL COMPLEMENT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE INVERSION SAFE FOR
// THE FLOAT COMPARES. A condition code is a predicate over one flag state; `invertX64Cond` and
// `invertArm64Cond` each map a code to the code that is true on exactly the flag states it is false
// on, so for ANY flags — arm64's `fcmp` NZCV=0b0011 on an unordered pair included — exactly one of
// the pair is taken. Swapping which target is branched to and which is fallen into therefore cannot
// change which arm a NaN reaches. It is NOT a "logical opposite" of the source-level predicate:
// `mi`'s complement is `pl`, which reads TRUE on NaN, and that is exactly right, because `mi`
// (float `<`) reads FALSE on NaN. `arm64Cbz`/`arm64Cbnz` complement each other on a register's
// zeroness and never read the flags at all.
//
// ⚠ ON arm64 THE INVERSION MOVES THE ELSE-EDGE ONTO THE 19-BIT `b.cond` (±1 MiB) AND THE THEN-EDGE
// ONTO A FALL-THROUGH. Either edge could already exceed that reach before this pass — the then-edge
// rode the same field — so the exposure is swapped rather than widened, and `arm64PatchBranch19`
// still refuses an out-of-reach displacement loudly.
function invertedCondBranchOnto(op TargetOp, targetBlockId BlockId, condOpIndex OpIndex) returns TargetOp
	return match op 'cond'
		jcc(cond, _) gives TargetOp.jcc(invertX64Cond(cond), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64CondBranch(cond, _) gives TargetOp.arm64CondBranch(invertArm64Cond(cond), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64Cbz(reg, _) gives TargetOp.arm64Cbnz(reg, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64Cbnz(reg, _) gives TargetOp.arm64Cbz(reg, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		default panic("invertedCondBranchOnto: op {condOpIndex} is recorded as a conditional branch but is not a `jcc`/`arm64CondBranch`/`arm64Cbz`/`arm64Cbnz`")
	end 'cond'
end 'invertedCondBranchOnto'

// Delete a block's terminator branch. `Terminator.fallthrough` is the slot's declared state for
// exactly this — *"terminator was deliberately elided at the target phase because the block falls
// straight through to the next block in layout order"* — and this pass is its only producer; both
// function-chunk emitters and the Target printer already emit nothing for a block that has no
// terminator OP. The branch itself is left where it lies in `module.ops`: nothing walks that array
// linearly, and rewriting every later op index to close the hole would cost far more than the word.
function elideTerminatorIn(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef)
	var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("elideTerminatorIn: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
	block.terminator = Terminator.fallthrough
end 'elideTerminatorIn'
